# Line endings are LF in the index and LF in every working tree, on every
# platform. Without this file the repo has NO attributes at all, so what lands
# in a Windows checkout is decided by that machine's `core.autocrlf` — a
# per-machine setting the repo can't see and CI doesn't share.
#
# Nothing was broken when this was added (issue #53's sweep): the index is 100%
# LF, the recorded Codex JSONL fixtures survive a trailing `\r` because JSON's
# grammar treats CR as whitespace, and no assertion in the suite is `$`-anchored
# or byte-for-byte against a file read off disk. The exposure is the NEXT test —
# a snapshot, an anchored regex, a fixture compared as bytes — which would fail
# only on Windows and only for whoever has `core.autocrlf=true`. That is a
# checkout-configuration problem, which is why `outputLines` in
# `tests/platform.ts` deliberately does not cover it: that helper is about what
# a native Windows binary writes to a PIPE, not what git writes to DISK.
#
# `text=auto` rather than a bare `text`, so git's own binary detection still
# runs and the tracked PNGs, WEBPs and WOFF2s stay untouched. `eol=lf` then
# fixes the checkout side for everything detection called text. No path wants
# CRLF: the repo ships no `.bat`, `.cmd` or `.ps1`, and the Windows entrypoints
# are `.mjs` and `.js`, which Node reads either way.
#
# Adding this cost nothing to land: `git ls-files --eol` reported `i/lf` for all
# 596 text files beforehand, so `git add --renormalize .` staged zero changes
# and no in-flight branch inherited a whole-tree conflict.
#
# `tests/gitattributes.test.ts` pins all three facts: that the attribute is
# still in effect, that binary detection still excludes the images and fonts,
# and — independently of either — that nothing is committed with CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf
